The test design is done: one tree with rows A1–A7, all seven automated. A1 and A6 also run by hand. It's saved to `.scratch/drop-unspeakable-units/test-design/api.md`.

- **The automated rows** poll a `generating` item whose extraction job resolves `complete` from a hand-built cassette. They cover:
  - the ornament drop (A1);
  - non-Latin text kept, and letters that exist only in markdown not saving a unit (A2);
  - an image moving up to the previous survivor, or to the front, when the text before it drops (A3, A4);
  - an item that continues with only an image (A5);
  - `extraction: no surviving units` when everything drops (A6);
  - a "Table of contents" alt now spoken as written (A7).
- **The two manual rows run entirely on the local stack.** Each spawns the extraction job straight on the local worker, with the fixture HTML and a recipe, and inserts a `generating` item holding that job's handle. A1 replays the real sockpuppet.org page and recipe, pulled from Cloudflare workflow `itm_3b033a2f`, and ends with a listen. A6 uses a hand-built page of ornaments only, run through the asermax.github.io recipe from `itm_0cb144c0`, and should fail with `extraction: no surviving units`.
- **Dropped:** the emphasis-restore case, because the restore goes by design, and a separate code-unit case, because a code unit's spoken form always has letters.

No implementation tasks or design changes came out of it. Nothing is committed: the docs changes from the previous step are still uncommitted in the working tree.
